{
  "case_id": "2019-19-46",
  "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m.\nAPPEARANCES:\nERICA L. ROSS, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioners.\nLISA S. BLATT, Esquire, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Yes, Ms. Ross. The -- a couple of questions.\nThe -- could Booking acquire an 800 number, for ex -- that's a vanity number, 1-800-booking, for example, that is similar to, for -- 1-800-plumbing, which is a registered mark?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ms. Ross, picking up on where you were right now, is it your position that the primary significance test to the public is never to be used in determining what's generic and what's not, or is it -- and if we don't use that test at least in whole or in part, however much you want, what other things would a PTO examiner look to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ross",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Sotomayor, it's not our position that the -- the courts and the PTO can never look to the primary significance test outside of cancellation. Again, I think that the reason why courts and the PTO do that is because the Lanham Act didn't overturn preexisting common law, and that, again, speaks to why we think Goodyear remains good law. But I think what courts should do in this instance is look to other sources to figure out what, again, a generic term -- whether"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm -- I'm right in saying that you're advocating for a categorical rule here, am I not? In other words, you're not saying that trademarks of this kind will rarely be registered -- registrable; you're saying that they will never be registrable. Is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ross",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it depends a little bit, Justice Kagan, on what you mean by \"trademarks of this kind.\" We think that when you're simply appending .com --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case is about how to tell the difference between descriptive names the Lanham Act protects and generic ones the Act does not. There are three reasons this Court should hold that the answer is the primary significance test, that is, whether consumers primarily think the name is a genus or a potential brand."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You rely heavily on the primary significance test, but that is only in the provision of the statute dealing with cancellation of marks. And, of course, this is not a cancellation case; it's a registration case. So why should we assume that the primary significance test carries the weight that you would give it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That -- that -- I don't think that can be right because, again, Goodyear is a -- Goodyear is a common law rule. Let me just talk about Goodyear. Goodyear is a common law rule that is based on the principle under common law that no mark could be trademarked if competitors could, with equal truth, hypothetically say that about the mark."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So do you make a -- the government relies on Goodyear, and you say that, of course, there's been a sea change in trademark law since Goodyear, particularly with the Lanham Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you have the same name, Ms. Blatt, then the consumers will know that the word com, .com, will get you a particular source, not all sellers of a given sort of commodity but one particular source."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What would your client's position be if companies that had -- that took Booking.com but made very slight variations sought trademark protection?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Are they required now to run their own consumer perception surveys before they determine that a particular name is generic?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If this is a factual question, and a district court is not to give the PTO deference, then what we're going to have is every district court reweighing all of this evidence that the PTO looked at."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Okay. Let me ask you another question that goes back to what Justice Alito said. And you said to him and to the Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But why people want trademark registration is twofold. Outright counterfeiting, because Booking.com is a -- is a popular name, and we don't want people ripping off or a store opening up."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Good morning, Ms. Blatt. I want to make sure I understand what you think about our precedent in Goodyear exactly. Could the principle of Goodyear still have some value outside the \".com\" context in the classic company context in which that case arose because the \".coms\" are inherently unique, or is your position more broadly that Goodyear just has no value anymore?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ross",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f4c3f3173af0-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 74,
        "start": 74
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- if I could just focus on three main points. First, I think Respondent's rule operates from the presumption that the Lanham Act knocked out all prior common law unless it was expressly preserved."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2019-19-46",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "f4c3f3173af07f0af8360a2540de16356c36266b5798e57bc795040ac55b18b9",
